    {"step": 1, "tactic": "P/Invoke compile of credui.dll!CredUIPromptForCredentialsW", "technique": "T1056.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-02-14 11:48:08 UTC on WS-MKT-08, AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe Add-Type compilation containing literal DllImport for credui.dll!CredUIPromptForCredentialsW AND the CREDUI_INFO struct definition (cbSize, hwndParent, pszMessageText, pszCaptionText, hbmBanner) AND ole32.dll!CoInitializeEx. Rule 4104 triggered.", "chain_reason": "CredUIPromptForCredentialsW is the Windows API that displays a native credential dialog. In a PowerShell scriptblock with a custom CREDUI_INFO struct, it has no legitimate administration use — corp credential prompts are issued by the OS shell or signed enterprise applications, not by user PowerShell."},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Build CREDUI_INFO with disguised Microsoft 365 caption", "technique": "T1056.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-02-14 11:48:18, AMSI EID 4104 captured construction of a CREDUI_INFO struct with pszCaptionText='Microsoft 365 Sign-in' AND pszMessageText='Outlook needs your password to continue.' AND target name 'Microsoft.AAD.BrokerPlugin'. The dialog will visually mimic a legitimate Office 365 / Azure AD sign-in prompt.", "chain_reason": "The caption and message are designed to look like a genuine Microsoft sign-in. The user — believing this is a normal Outlook re-authentication prompt — will type their corp credentials, which the calling PowerShell process captures via the password StringBuilder."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Sysmon EID 7 credui.dll loaded into powershell.exe", "technique": "T1056.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-02-14 11:48:24, Sysmon EID 7 (Image Loaded) recorded credui.dll loaded into powershell.exe. Rule 92207 triggered. credui.dll is normally loaded only by svchost.exe (SSPI) and rundll32.exe with specific Windows-issued commands.", "chain_reason": "credui.dll in a PowerShell process means PowerShell is preparing to display a credential dialog. Combined with Q1 (CredUIPromptForCredentialsW import) and Q2 (M365 disguised caption), this is the credential-phishing primitive about to fire."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Capture log + exfiltration POST to attacker C2", "technique": "T1056.002", "what_happened": "At 2033-02-14 11:48:38, Sysmon EID 11 captured creation of C:\\Users\\e.morales\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\p138_credui_114808\\stolen.log containing the header 'target: Microsoft.AAD.BrokerPlugin / caption: Microsoft 365 Sign-in / username: ... / password: ...'. At 2033-02-14 11:48:48, Sysmon EID 3 recorded an outbound HTTPS POST from powershell.exe to a non-corp destination 10.108.57.10 (test target) with UA 'credui-exfil/1.0' and request body containing the stolen.log content. Rule 4104 secondary triggered.", "chain_reason": "The stolen.log file + POST with UA 'credui-exfil/1.0' to a non-corp IP IS the credential exfiltration step. The 'credui-exfil/1.0' UA is operator-named transparent attacker fingerprint."}
